    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n19034" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">fundāmen</span></b> , ĭnis,
			n.
		2. fundo, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>a foundation</i> (<b>poet.</b> for
			the class. fundamentum; mostly in plur.): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">ponere fundamina,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Verg.
					G. 4, 161</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Siculae terrae,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Ov. M.
					5, 361</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">rerum,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id.
					ib. 15, 433</span></i>
			; <i><span class="en">id. F. 4, 835</span></i>.—In
				sing.: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">fundamine magno res Romana valet,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Ov.
					M. 14, 808</span></i>
			; <i><span class="en">Hier. Gal. 4, 1</span></i>;
			<i><span class="en">2</span></i>.</div>
